{"blocks":[["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1041","Fast Access: Stack memory is typically faster to access than heap memory",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1122","Limited Size: Stack size is usually smaller and more constrained than heap",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1205","Scope-Based Lifetime: Variables live only within their defining scope",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1283","Predictable Layout: Stack frames are organized in a predictable, sequential manner",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1374","Mechanism",["Stack Conceptual Overview"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1390","When a function is called:",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1421","A new stack frame is pushed onto the stack",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function is called:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1469","Local variables and parameters are stored in this frame",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function is called:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1530","Return address is stored for when the function completes",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function is called:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1592","When a function returns:",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1621","The stack frame is popped",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function returns:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1652","All local variables in that frame are automatically deallocated",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function returns:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1721","Execution returns to the calling function",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","When a function returns:"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1768","Stack pointer tracks the current top of the stack",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1822","Stack overflow occurs when the stack grows beyond its allocated size (often from deep recursion)",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1923","Examples",["Stack Conceptual Overview"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1938","Function Calls",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-1961","Each function call creates a new stack frame",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Function Calls"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2011","Local variables are stored in the current stack frame",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Function Calls"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2070","When the function returns, its frame is removed",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Function Calls"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2123","Recursion",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2141","Each recursive call adds a new stack frame",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Recursion"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2189","Deep recursion can cause stack overflow",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Recursion"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2234","Common Stack-Allocated Types",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2271","Local variables",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Common Stack-Allocated Types"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2292","Function parameters",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Common Stack-Allocated Types"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2317","Return addresses",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Common Stack-Allocated Types"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2339","Small, fixed-size data structures",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Common Stack-Allocated Types"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2378","Language Examples",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2404","C/C++: Local variables are typically stack-allocated",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Language Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2466","Rust: Most values are stack-allocated by default",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Language Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2524","Python: Function call frames use a stack structure",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Examples","Language Examples"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2584","Misconceptions",["Stack Conceptual Overview"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2605","Stack is always faster than heap → Generally true, but context matters. Stack is faster for small, short-lived data",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2731","All variables go on the stack → False. Large or dynamically-sized data often goes on the heap",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2835","Stack size is unlimited → False. Stack size is limited and stack overflow is a real concern",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-2937","Stack and heap are physical locations → False. They are logical memory regions with different management strategies",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-3063","Stack frames are only for function calls → False. Stack frames can also contain exception handling information and other execution context",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-3212","Related",["Stack Conceptual Overview"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-3226","Programming/Concept/Heap - The heap, which contrasts with stack memory",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-3305","Rust/Variable - How Rust uses the stack for variable storage",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-9662d0843e2ca4ae.html","source-block-3374","Programming/Time/Run - Runtime behavior including stack management",["Stack Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-74","vs (Diátaxis overview)",[]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-129","Conceptual overview",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-155","State is information a system remembers between one interaction and the next.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Conceptual overview"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-239","A component is stateful if its behavior depends on data carried over from prior interactions, and stateless if each interaction is handled purely from the inputs it is given right now.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Conceptual overview"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-436","The distinction is relative to a boundary: the same overall system is usually stateless at one layer by pushing its state down to another (a database, cache, token, or client).",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Conceptual overview"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-621","Key idea",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-636","Stateful = the component itself remembers; identity and history live inside it.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Key idea"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-726","Stateless = the component remembers nothing; everything it needs arrives with the request, and state (if any) lives outside it.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Key idea"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-864","\"Stateless\" almost never means \"no state anywhere\" — it means \"no state retained here between calls\".",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Key idea"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-974","Stateful",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-989","Mental model",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1006","Each interaction can read and mutate memory left behind by previous interactions.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Mental model"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1093","The component has an identity that must be tracked: which session, which connection, which instance.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Mental model"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1199","Properties",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1214","Context is implicit — the caller can send less because the component already \"knows where we were\".",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1321","Affinity: a client must often keep talking to the same instance that holds its state (sticky sessions).",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1432","Recovery is harder — if the instance dies, the in-memory state dies with it unless it was persisted.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1540","Typical examples",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1561","TCP connections (sequence numbers, window state)",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1615","A shell session (cwd, environment, variables)",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1666","An in-memory shopping cart tied to one server process",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1725","A long-lived agent/runtime that accumulates working memory across turns",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateful","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1802","Stateless",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1818","Mental model",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1835","Treat each request as if it were the first the component has ever seen.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Mental model"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-1912","Same inputs → same behavior, regardless of what came before (closely related to a pure function).",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Mental model"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2019","Properties",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2034","Context is explicit — every request must carry whatever the handler needs (auth token, ids, parameters).",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2146","No affinity — any instance can serve any request, because none of them hold private history.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2246","Recovery and scaling are easy — instances are interchangeable and disposable; restart or add more freely.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Properties"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2359","Typical examples",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2380","HTTP as a protocol (each request is self-contained)",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2437","REST endpoints (state, if any, lives in the resource store, not the handler)",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2519","Pure functions",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2539","A CDN edge node or load-balanced web worker behind a shared datastore",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Stateless","Typical examples"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2614","Trade-offs",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2631","Scaling",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2647","Stateless scales horizontally almost for free: put N identical instances behind a load balancer.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Scaling"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2749","Stateful resists horizontal scaling because requests must be routed to the instance that owns the state, or that state must be replicated.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Scaling"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2893","Recovery & resilience",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2923","Stateless instances are disposable — crash one and route around it.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Recovery & resilience"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-2998","Stateful instances are precious — losing one loses data unless state was externalized or replicated.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Recovery & resilience"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3106","Performance & cost",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3133","Stateful can be faster per request: the context is already in memory, nothing to refetch.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Performance & cost"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3228","Stateless pays a re-establishment cost each call (re-send context, re-read from the store) in exchange for elasticity.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Performance & cost"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3352","Complexity location",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3380","The choice does not delete state — it relocates it.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Complexity location"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3439","Going stateless usually means externalizing state into a database, cache, or a token the client carries, which moves the hard problems (consistency, expiry, size) to that layer.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Trade-offs","Complexity location"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3624","Examples (concrete contrasts)",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3660","TCP vs HTTP",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3680","TCP is stateful: both ends track connection state for the life of the connection.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","TCP vs HTTP"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3771","HTTP (the protocol) is stateless: each request stands alone; continuity is faked with cookies/tokens that re-supply context.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","TCP vs HTTP"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3905","REST",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-3918","A core REST constraint is statelessness: the server keeps no client session; each request includes everything needed to interpret it.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","REST"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4057","The application still has state — it lives in the resources/database, not in the request handler.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","REST"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4164","Pure vs impure functions",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4197","A pure function is stateless: output depends only on arguments.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","Pure vs impure functions"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4266","A function reading/writing shared mutable state is stateful: the same arguments can yield different results over time.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Examples (concrete contrasts)","Pure vs impure functions"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4390","Practical heuristics",["vs (Diátaxis overview)"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4417","Ask \"if this instance vanished mid-request, what is lost?\" — if the answer is \"nothing the next instance can't reconstruct from inputs/store\", it's effectively stateless.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Practical heuristics"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4594","Prefer stateless components at the edges (web tier, agents, workers) and concentrate necessary state in a small number of deliberately stateful stores.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Practical heuristics"]],["p-56a2fb9642e8ac01.html","source-block-4750","When something \"must\" be stateful, make the state explicit and externalizable rather than hidden in instance memory — it makes scaling and recovery a configuration choice rather than a rewrite.",["vs (Diátaxis overview)","Practical heuristics"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-0","Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview",[]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-52","Overview",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-67","Virtualization and Emulation both let one machine run software intended for another environment, but they optimize for different outcomes.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-218","Virtualization prioritizes near-native performance by reusing the host CPU architecture.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-311","Emulation prioritizes compatibility by reproducing a different CPU or hardware model in software.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-413","Context",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-427","Teams often need to run workloads that were built for different operating systems, kernel assumptions, or processor architectures.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-562","The practical question is usually: \"Do I need speed on similar hardware, or compatibility across different hardware?\"",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-684","Key Principles",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-705","Instruction path",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-730","Virtualization executes most guest instructions directly on host hardware.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Instruction path"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-810","Emulation translates or interprets guest instructions for a different target architecture.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Instruction path"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-906","Architecture coupling",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-936","Virtualization is strongest when guest and host CPU families match (for example, ARM on ARM or x86 on x86).",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Architecture coupling"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1049","Emulation is used when guest and host CPU families differ (for example, x86 guest on ARM host).",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Architecture coupling"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1150","Performance profile",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1178","Virtualization usually has lower overhead.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Performance profile"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1226","Emulation usually has higher overhead because of instruction translation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Performance profile"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1305","Fidelity goals",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1328","Virtualization models an isolated machine boundary.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Fidelity goals"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1385","Emulation models hardware behavior and instruction semantics.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Fidelity goals"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1452","Mechanism",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1468","Virtualization",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1491","A hypervisor coordinates privileged operations, memory mapping, and device access while letting most CPU instructions run natively.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Virtualization"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1628","Hardware extensions (for example VT-x or ARM virtualization support) reduce trap-and-emulate overhead.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Virtualization"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1736","Emulation",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1754","An emulator decodes guest instructions and maps them into host operations (interpreter or dynamic binary translation).",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Emulation"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1878","Device models and firmware behavior are reproduced in software to match target expectations.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Emulation"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1976","Examples",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-1991","Running a Linux ARM64 VM on an Apple Silicon Mac with hardware acceleration is primarily virtualization.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2100","Running legacy x86 software on an ARM-only host without native x86 hardware support requires emulation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2208","Hybrid systems (for example UTM via ) may use virtualization when architectures match and emulation when they do not.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2342","Misconceptions",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2363","Virtualization and emulation are the same thing -> False. They can look similar at the UI level, but the execution model is different.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2506","Emulation is always unacceptable for real work -> False. It can be essential for compatibility testing, reverse engineering, and legacy workloads.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2661","Virtualization always works for any guest OS on any host -> False. Cross-architecture scenarios generally require emulation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2794","Related",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2808","UTM",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-f07db4e4e2f7c416.html","source-block-2833","Container",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-0","Destructuring",[]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-18","Also known as: unpacking, destructuring assignment",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-72","A programming concept that allows unpacking values from data structures (arrays, objects, tuples, structs) into distinct variables",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-206","Simplifies the extraction of multiple values and enhances code readability",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-284","Overview",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-299","Enables developers to extract multiple values from data structures in a concise and readable manner",["Destructuring","Overview"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-403","Particularly useful when dealing with complex data structures or when functions return multiple values",["Destructuring","Overview"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-510","Reduces boilerplate code when accessing multiple properties or elements",["Destructuring","Overview"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-586","Language Implementations",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-617","JavaScript",["Destructuring","Language Implementations"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-636","Destructuring assignment allows unpacking values from arrays or properties from objects",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","JavaScript"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-729","Array destructuring:",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","JavaScript"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-823","Object destructuring:",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","JavaScript"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-936","Python",["Destructuring","Language Implementations"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-951","Tuple unpacking enables assigning values from tuples or lists to variables",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Python"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1031","Tuple unpacking:",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Python"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1108","Dictionary unpacking (using unpacking operator):",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Python"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1349","Rust",["Destructuring","Language Implementations"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1362","Pattern matching facilitates destructuring of tuples, structs, and enums",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Rust"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1440","Tuple destructuring:",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Rust"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1517","Struct destructuring:",["Destructuring","Language Implementations","Rust"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1680","Benefits",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1695","Improved Readability",["Destructuring","Benefits"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1724","Provides a clear and concise way to extract values",["Destructuring","Benefits","Improved Readability"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1780","Makes code easier to understand",["Destructuring","Benefits","Improved Readability"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1817","Reduced Boilerplate",["Destructuring","Benefits"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1845","Eliminates the need for repetitive code when accessing multiple properties or elements",["Destructuring","Benefits","Reduced Boilerplate"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1937","Reduces verbosity in variable assignments",["Destructuring","Benefits","Reduced Boilerplate"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-1984","Enhanced Maintainability",["Destructuring","Benefits"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2017","Simplifies variable assignments",["Destructuring","Benefits","Enhanced Maintainability"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2054","Makes code maintenance more straightforward",["Destructuring","Benefits","Enhanced Maintainability"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2103","Advanced Features",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2127","Default Values",["Destructuring","Advanced Features"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2150","Some languages allow setting default values during destructuring to handle undefined or missing properties",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Default Values"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2262","Example (JavaScript):",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Default Values"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2371","Nested Destructuring",["Destructuring","Advanced Features"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2400","Can be applied to nested data structures",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Nested Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2446","May increase complexity and reduce readability if overused",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Nested Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2510","Example (JavaScript):",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Nested Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2613","Rest/Spread Patterns",["Destructuring","Advanced Features"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2642","Some languages support collecting remaining elements",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Rest/Spread Patterns"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2700","Example (JavaScript):",["Destructuring","Advanced Features","Rest/Spread Patterns"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2802","Related",["Destructuring"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2816","Programming/Pattern Matching - Destructuring is often used with pattern matching",["Destructuring","Related"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2905","Rust/Variable/Type/Compound/Tuple - Tuple destructuring in Rust",["Destructuring","Related"]],["p-572878e8313bb68e.html","source-block-2977","Variable assignment and binding",["Destructuring","Related"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-111","Dynamic Programming Reference",[]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-145","Overview",["Dynamic Programming Reference"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-160","Dynamic programming is an algorithmic paradigm that solves complex problems by breaking them down into simpler subproblems",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Overview"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-287","It stores the results of subproblems to avoid redundant computations, trading space for time",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Overview"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-384","The approach is particularly effective for optimization problems with overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Overview"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-507","Core Principles",["Dynamic Programming Reference"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-529","Optimal Substructure",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-558","An optimal solution to a problem contains optimal solutions to its subproblems",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Optimal Substructure"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-642","Example: In shortest path problems, the optimal path from A to C contains the optimal path from A to B and B to C",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Optimal Substructure"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-761","Mathematical formulation: If is optimal for problem , then any sub-solution is optimal for subproblem",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Optimal Substructure"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-899","Overlapping Subproblems",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-931","The same subproblems are solved multiple times during the computation",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Overlapping Subproblems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1006","Dynamic programming stores these results to avoid recomputation",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Overlapping Subproblems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1075","Example: Fibonacci sequence calculation reuses and multiple times",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Overlapping Subproblems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1168","Without DP: computes twice, three times, five times",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Core Principles","Overlapping Subproblems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1261","Implementation Approaches",["Dynamic Programming Reference"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1293","Top-Down (Memoization)",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1324","Recursive approach with caching of computed results",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Top-Down (Memoization)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1381","Starts from the original problem and works down to base cases",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Top-Down (Memoization)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1448","Uses a memo table to store intermediate results",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Top-Down (Memoization)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1501","Example: Recursive Fibonacci with memoization",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Top-Down (Memoization)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1779","Bottom-Up (Tabulation)",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1810","Iterative approach that builds solutions from base cases up",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Bottom-Up (Tabulation)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1875","Fills a table systematically, ensuring all dependencies are computed first",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Bottom-Up (Tabulation)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-1955","Generally more space-efficient than top-down approaches",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Bottom-Up (Tabulation)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2016","Example: Iterative Fibonacci using an array",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Implementation Approaches","Bottom-Up (Tabulation)"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2289","Classic Problems",["Dynamic Programming Reference"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2312","Fibonacci Sequence",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2339","Problem: Calculate where , ,",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems","Fibonacci Sequence"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2433","Time complexity: with DP vs with naive recursion",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems","Fibonacci Sequence"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2507","Space complexity: with bottom-up, with top-down",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems","Fibonacci Sequence"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2578","Mathematical recurrence: for",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems","Fibonacci Sequence"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2652","Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems"]],["p-e1f9864d7420c718.html","source-block-2693","Problem: Find the longest subsequence common to two strings",["Dynamic Programming Reference","Classic Problems","Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)"]],["p-e